    {
      "title": "Apple defends Google Search deal in court: 'There wasn't a valid alternative'",
      "summary": "Eddy Cue testified in US v. Google that Apple had no valid alternative to Google's search engine and negotiated the 2016 Information Services Agreement (ISA) with Sundar Pichai.",
      "content": "Eddy Cue testified in US v. Google, the antitrust trial over Google's search business, defending the Information Services Agreement (ISA) which makes Google the default search engine on Apple devices.\n\nThe ISA has been in place since 2002. In 2016, Cue negotiated the current iteration with Google CEO Sundar Pichai.\n\nDuring renegotiation, Apple wanted a higher percentage of revenue from users directed to the search engine, while Pichai wanted to keep the deal as is. They compromised on an undisclosed number.\n\nCue stated there was no valid alternative to Google at the time and there still isn't one. He argued the deal was about more than economics and that Apple never seriously considered switching providers or building its own search product."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "notes": [
    {
      "title": "Cue on ISA search engine choice",
      "summary": "Cue acknowledged the ISA didn't allow Apple to offer search engine choices during setup, citing speed and user experience as reasons for defaulting to Google."
    },
    {
      "title": "Cue on Google privacy claims",
      "summary": "Cue agreed Apple believes user privacy is important and claimed Apple had better privacy than Google, citing ISA provisions allowing login-free searches and anti-tracking measures."
    },
    {
      "title": "Bellshaw on ISA profit implications",
      "summary": "Bellshaw questioned if the ISA represents a significant portion of Apple's profits; Cue replied Apple does not view it that way because the platform's appeal makes the agreement successful."
    },
    {
      "title": "Schmidtlein on Safari and Google history",
      "summary": "Schmidtlein led Cue through Safari's history, noting Google integration was a user interface innovation and was nearly always mentioned in pre-2003 promotional materials."
    },
    {
      "title": "Cue on pre-2003 search behavior",
      "summary": "Cue stated that before 2003, users had to type google.com or another URL to search, whereas Apple's system redirects non-URL text to search."
    },
    {
      "title": "Schmidtlein on search engine switching",
      "summary": "Schmidtlein and Cue argued Google helped Safari succeed by being a great product and that switching search engines is easy, referencing Apple's deals with Yahoo and Bing."
    },
    {
      "title": "DOJ questions on Google market power",
      "summary": "The DOJ questions if Google is the best search engine or just pays the most, and what the market looks like without those payments; Cue said Apple never considered this."
    }
  ]
}

